From 5a762151ae80a88f973e845da2fa4655f4472269 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: csolomon <charliesolomon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:24:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] offline-model-data: build without the vendor catalogue fetch

packages/ai's build ran generate-models --strict, which hydrates the model
catalogue from ~20 vendor APIs and makes any single failure fatal. GAH
denies that catalogue at runtime (0010-restrict-model-sources), so a fresh
clone paid for a fetch it could not use -- and behind a filtering proxy
could not build at all (#14, #15). build:offline was no escape: it
validates a hydration a clone does not have, because upstream gitignores
packages/ai/src/providers/data/ while tracking the 39 shards that import it.

Three changes, one concern:

1. scripts/gah-model-data.ts (new file, never conflicts) materialises
   src/providers/data/ with no network: every provider shard the tracked
   aggregator imports gets a data file, copied verbatim from a seed
   directory when one has it and an empty catalogue ({}) otherwise, plus
   the manifest check:model-data expects. The seed is $GAH_MODEL_DATA_DIR,
   else <gah checkout>/packages/policy-pack/model-data, else none. A seed
   file with no matching shard fails the build rather than being ignored,
   so an upstream provider rename is heard about.

2. scripts/model-data.ts: readProviderStructure no longer rejects a
   provider file with zero models. An empty catalogue is the intended
   state for every provider GAH does not seed; the check existed to catch
   a botched hydration, which the generator still guards with --strict.

3. package.json: `build` runs gah:model-data instead of generate-models.
   generate-models, hydrate-model-data and build:offline are untouched so
   maintainers can still hydrate with network to refresh the seed.

Why the seed exists at all, rather than shipping everything empty as #15
sketched: the shared host allowlists amazon-bedrock/us.anthropic.* out of
the built-in catalogue (deploy/host/users.d/agent.conf.example), and
Bedrock's AWS credential-chain auth cannot be re-created through
registerProvider, so those models must exist in the built-in data for the
allowlist to match anything. GAH_BUILTIN_MODELS still filters at runtime;
seeding a provider exposes nothing by itself.

Why a patch and not a policy-pack extension: the data files are imported
by the provider shards at compile time and must exist before tsgo runs;
nothing that loads at runtime can supply them. Kept small on purpose --
generate-models.ts (3000 lines, rewritten every release) is not touched.

Verified from a clean data directory with globalThis.fetch stubbed to
throw: the full nine-package `npm run build` completes in ~10s;
dist/providers/data holds two non-empty files (amazon-bedrock, anthropic)
and 37 `{}`; --list-models shows the seeded models under their allowlists
and nothing for an emptied provider. Upstream's catalogue-content tests
(providers.test.ts, model-catalog-types.test.ts) assume a full hydration
and fail against the emptied providers; this repo's CI does not run them,
and they pass again after `npm run hydrate:model-data`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011it956DeV4unyWw5VyvkDD
